// SECURITY -- LIVE CAPABILITY ESCAPE, confirmed. RED TEAM round 3,
// cap-escape lens (2026-08-04). Prior actor probes (attack_wrap_closure_
// actor.kry, cap_escape_decoy_actor_message.kry) tested (a) an actor
// method WRAPPING a received closure into a NEW lambda and returning it
// (blocked, partly on an orthogonal non-unit-return rule), and (b) a
// decoy-companion shape where BOTH the vulnerable call and its containers
// live inside a single actor-method invocation (`w.run(decoy, real)`,
// itself an ordinary synchronous call). Neither probed a THIRD shape: the
// privileged closure is STORED into the actor's own private state field by
// one fire-and-forget dispatch (`h.stash(reader)`, unit return -- so the
// E0110 non-unit-return rule that blocked the wrap-return shape does not
// apply), and a SEPARATE, independently-dispatched zero-cap message later
// reads that field and invokes it directly from inside the actor's own
// method body (`h.invoke()`). Ordinary struct-field mutation-then-read
// through this exact shape is already CLOSED (see
// cap_escape_closure_launder_field_mutate.kry -- E0507 fail-closed) --
// actor state is NOT ordinary struct-field storage this checker tracks the
// same way, and it silently defeats `deny!()`.
//
// VERIFIED LIVE against `compiler/target/release/kryos.exe`, HEAD
// `00b3cf7`, no compiler changes, both `kryos run` and
// `kryos check --strict-capabilities`, reproduced twice (deterministic --
// this is a static compile-time check, not a race):
//
//   $ kryos run attack_actor_state_stored_closure.kry
//   ACTOR-STATE-STORED (was NOT CLOSED): TOPSECRET-CLOSURE-9f8e7d6c5b4a
//   $ echo $?
//   0
//   $ kryos check --strict-capabilities attack_actor_state_stored_closure.kry
//   $ echo $?
//   0
//
// Decisive control (not vacuous): swapping `reader` for a zero-capability
// decoy closure in the identical actor shape compiles and runs identically
// clean ("CONTROL (decoy, expected no error): decoy-no-secret", exit 0) --
// so the shape is legitimately capability-clean when the stored closure
// really is zero-cap, and the ONLY reason this file should fail is the
// real `fs:read` authority carried by `reader`, which `deny!(fs:read)` is
// supposed to strip and does not.
//
// ACCEPTANCE (not yet met): must be REJECTED (E0507) under both
// `--capabilities-mode=inferred` (default) and `--strict-capabilities`.

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn make_secret_reader(path: str) -> fn() -> str {
    return || file_read(path)
}

actor Holder {
    reader: fn() -> str

    fn stash(self, r: fn() -> str) {
        self.reader = r
    }

    fn invoke(self) {
        let leaked = self.reader()
        println("ACTOR-STATE-STORED (was NOT CLOSED): " + leaked)
    }
}

@capabilities(fs:read)
fn main() {
    let reader = make_secret_reader("tests/security/secret_for_closure_launder.txt")
    let h = Holder()
    h.stash(reader)
    deny!(fs:read) {
        h.invoke()
        sleep(100)
    }
}
